WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.284
Welcome to section nine, trauma.

2
00:00:03.284 --> 00:00:05.198
The exam here is wide.

3
00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:35.210
We will build it on the primary survey, ABCDE, and then focus on the respiratory high-yields: pneumothorax recognition and treatment, chest tubes, the Glasgow Coma Scale, the MAP minus ICP relationship that drives cerebral perfusion, imaging choices, blunt versus penetrating injury, head-injury priorities, and postoperative pulmonary embolism.

4
00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:45.475
A running theme for the RT: avoid hypoxemia, avoid excessive swings in PaCO2, and help prevent secondary brain injury.

5
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:55.015
The Trauma deck defines a pneumothorax as air in the pleural space that can cause the lung to collapse.

6
00:00:55.015 --> 00:01:06.992
It is one of the most common and potentially lethal injuries in chest trauma: about twenty percent of patients who reach a trauma center alive have one.

7
00:01:06.992 --> 00:01:11.877
There are three subtypes: simple or closed, open, and tension.

8
00:01:11.877 --> 00:01:25.350
The chest-tube deck lists these causes: positive-pressure ventilation, a ruptured bleb in emphysema, a bronchopleural fistula, a leaking subpleural cyst, and chest trauma.

9
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:35.750
Iatrogenic causes are thoracentesis, central vein and pulmonary artery catheterization, and bronchoscopy with transbronchial biopsy.

10
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:46.439
Bedside findings: chest pain and respiratory distress with diminished or absent breath sounds on the affected side.

11
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:53.777
Percussion is hyperresonant, fremitus is decreased, and expansion is decreased on one side.

12
00:01:53.777 --> 00:02:04.017
Subcutaneous emphysema, the crackling crepitus under the skin, means an air leak but does not by itself confirm a pneumothorax.

13
00:02:04.017 --> 00:02:12.484
Tracheal deviation away from the affected side is classic but late and unreliable, so do not wait for it.

14
00:02:12.484 --> 00:02:21.434
Hypotension with respiratory distress and unilateral diminished breath sounds should raise concern for tension.

15
00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:25.143
A chest x-ray confirms it in a stable patient.

16
00:02:25.143 --> 00:02:32.642
On the ventilator, a pneumothorax raises both peak and plateau pressures as compliance drops.

17
00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:45.344
Simple pneumothorax: air collects without progressive pressure buildup; small ones may resolve on their own, and larger ones may need a chest tube.

18
00:02:45.344 --> 00:02:59.614
Open, the sucking chest wound: a chest-wall defect lets air move with each breath; place a sterile occlusive dressing, often taped on three sides, then a chest tube and surgical closure.

19
00:02:59.614 --> 00:03:12.886
Tension: air enters but cannot escape, pressure builds, the mediastinum may shift, venous return and cardiac output fall, and obstructive shock or cardiac arrest can follow.

20
00:03:12.886 --> 00:03:24.317
When tension is suspected with hemodynamic instability, decompress immediately without waiting for a chest x-ray, then place a definitive chest tube.

21
00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:33.621
Needle decompression is lifesaving but temporary; the definitive treatment is a chest tube.

22
00:03:33.621 --> 00:03:42.463
A fourteen-gauge catheter is commonly taught, with size, length, and site following current protocols.

23
00:03:42.463 --> 00:03:49.310
The traditional site is the second intercostal space in the midclavicular line.

24
00:03:49.310 --> 00:04:02.313
Adult trauma protocols often prefer the fourth to fifth intercostal space anterior to the midaxillary region because catheter failure rates are lower.

25
00:04:02.313 --> 00:04:11.241
Hess adds that about thirty percent of trauma patients have a chest wall thicker than five centimeters.

26
00:04:11.241 --> 00:04:27.883
The RCP 105 deck calls untreated tension pneumothorax the only absolute physiologic contraindication to positive-pressure ventilation, so decompress before or at the same time as starting PPV.

27
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:35.034
The GCS provides the most objective assessment of consciousness.

28
00:04:35.034 --> 00:04:50.086
It adds eye, verbal, and motor scores: eye opening up to four, verbal response up to five, motor response up to six, giving a maximum of fifteen and a minimum of three.

29
00:04:50.086 --> 00:04:56.716
The 203 deck calls three deep coma, and Hess says it suggests brain death.

30
00:04:56.716 --> 00:05:04.601
Severity bands: mild thirteen to fifteen, moderate nine to twelve, severe eight or less.

31
00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:14.725
One RCP 105 slide prints thirteen to fourteen and less than eight, but use thirteen to fifteen and eight or less.

32
00:05:14.725 --> 00:05:20.011
Motor response is the best predictor of neurologic outcome.

33
00:05:20.011 --> 00:05:28.075
In an intubated patient, verbal cannot be assessed, so the score runs from two-T to ten-T.

34
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.907
Pause and think.

35
00:05:30.907 --> 00:05:38.996
A trauma patient opens eyes to pain, makes incomprehensible sounds, and withdraws from pain.

36
00:05:38.996 --> 00:05:39.875
Add it up.

37
00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:50.250
What is the overall score, what severity band is it, and what single airway decision does the deck tie to that number?

38
00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:57.167
[6-second practice pause]

39
00:05:57.667 --> 00:06:03.472
Eye to pain is two, incomprehensible sounds is two, withdrawal is four.

40
00:06:03.472 --> 00:06:06.416
Two plus two plus four equals eight.

41
00:06:06.416 --> 00:06:14.674
Eight sits at the top of the severe band, and the deck's rule is GCS of eight or less means intubate.

42
00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:27.430
We intubate not because the patient cannot breathe but because the patient cannot protect the airway, cough, handle secretions, or guard against aspiration.

43
00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:41.167
Remember the exceptions: intoxication, post-ictal states, and heavy sedation can give a temporary low GCS without severe brain injury, so clinical judgment still rules.

44
00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:47.798
Mean arterial pressure is the systemic driving pressure.

45
00:06:47.798 --> 00:06:55.177
The 203 deck writes it as diastolic plus one-third of systolic minus diastolic.

46
00:06:55.177 --> 00:07:05.265
For a blood pressure of one-ten over seventy, that is seventy plus thirteen point three, about eighty-three.

47
00:07:05.265 --> 00:07:10.963
Intracranial pressure is the pressure inside the rigid skull.

48
00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:33.101
The Monro-Kellie doctrine says the total volume of brain, CSF, and venous blood must stay constant, so if blood is added, CSF and venous blood must leave or ICP rises; Hess adds that once compensation is used up, ICP rises exponentially.

49
00:07:33.101 --> 00:07:37.678
Cerebral perfusion pressure equals MAP minus ICP.

50
00:07:37.678 --> 00:07:45.992
If MAP falls or ICP rises, CPP falls, cerebral blood flow drops, and ischemia can follow.

51
00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:58.992
The Trauma deck goal is CPP above sixty millimeters of mercury, reached by lowering ICP, raising MAP, or both, and Hess agrees.

52
00:07:58.992 --> 00:08:14.508
The RCP 105 decks call seventy to eighty normal and seventy their critical threshold; for a trauma question, use sixty, but recognize seventy if an answer choice uses it.

53
00:08:14.508 --> 00:08:20.350
Normal ICP differs between decks; RCP 105 gives eight to twelve.

54
00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:28.290
Every deck that gives a number agrees ICP above twenty is abnormal and needs treatment.

55
00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:43.624
What raises ICP: edema, hemorrhage, high intrathoracic pressure from PEEP or recruitment, hypercapnia, hypoxia, Trendelenburg, neck flexion, suctioning, and asynchrony.

56
00:08:43.624 --> 00:08:51.200
What lowers it: head elevation, less CSF, and hyperventilation; Hess adds mannitol.

57
00:08:52.600 --> 00:09:04.712
CT uses x-rays from multiple angles to build cross-sectional slices, with gray levels in Hounsfield units, and it uses ionizing radiation.

58
00:09:04.712 --> 00:09:22.091
The Trauma deck calls chest CT the best modality for the hemodynamically stable patient with a chest injury; it finds injuries a chest x-ray misses, such as small pneumothoraces and vascular injury.

59
00:09:22.091 --> 00:09:28.235
Unstable patients should not have lifesaving treatment delayed for CT.

60
00:09:28.235 --> 00:09:35.432
Hess adds that non-contrast head CT characterizes most early intracranial lesions.

61
00:09:35.432 --> 00:09:44.824
MRI uses a strong magnetic field and radio-frequency pulses on hydrogen nuclei, with no ionizing radiation.

62
00:09:44.824 --> 00:09:50.353
Diffuse axonal injury is hard to see on CT and may require MRI.

63
00:09:50.353 --> 00:10:01.500
MRI limits monitoring and needs compatible equipment and aluminum oxygen cylinders, because steel cylinders become projectiles.

64
00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:11.415
Place a chest tube any time air or fluid in the pleural space keeps the lung from fully expanding.

65
00:10:11.415 --> 00:10:23.358
The RCP 105 indications are a large tension pneumothorax over twenty-five percent, hemothorax, pleural effusion, empyema, and chylothorax.

66
00:10:23.358 --> 00:10:40.494
Match size to contents: for an adult pneumothorax, sixteen to twenty French may be adequate, while blood or thick fluid gets about thirty-six French, range twenty-eight to forty, to reduce clotting.

67
00:10:40.494 --> 00:10:44.129
Think of a milkshake versus a water straw.

68
00:10:44.129 --> 00:10:48.024
Large adults take thirty-six to forty French.

69
00:10:48.024 --> 00:10:56.245
The safe triangle is the fourth to fifth intercostal space at the anterior-to-midaxillary line.

70
00:10:56.245 --> 00:11:03.948
Air rises, so aim the tube up toward the apex; fluid falls, so aim it down and posterior.

71
00:11:03.948 --> 00:11:13.208
Insert over the top of the rib, because the intercostal vein, artery, and nerve run along its lower border.

72
00:11:14.633 --> 00:11:16.975
Three chambers, three jobs.

73
00:11:16.975 --> 00:11:21.139
One, collection, collects and measures drainage.

74
00:11:21.139 --> 00:11:30.593
Two, the water seal holds about two centimeters of water as a one-way valve, so air leaves but cannot return.

75
00:11:30.593 --> 00:11:44.038
Three, suction control: the water level, not the wall vacuum, sets the suction; the RCP 105 deck recommends minus ten to minus twenty centimeters of water.

76
00:11:44.038 --> 00:11:54.013
Gentle continuous bubbling in the suction chamber is normal; vigorous bubbling only means wall suction is too high.

77
00:11:54.013 --> 00:12:06.851
Tidaling in the water seal, rising on spontaneous inspiration and falling on expiration, means the system still communicates with the pleural space.

78
00:12:06.851 --> 00:12:21.683
Intermittent bubbling with exhalation or cough is an air leak, expected while a pneumothorax is evacuating; continuous bubbling means a large leak or a leak in the system.

79
00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:37.655
Unexpected loss of tidaling suggests a kinked or blocked tube: check for kinks and dependent loops, milk the tubing, and if that fails, check the patient and notify the physician.

80
00:12:37.655 --> 00:12:42.452
For continuous bubbling, clamp briefly near the chest wall.

81
00:12:42.452 --> 00:12:51.397
If bubbling stops, the leak is likely from the patient; get a chest x-ray as ordered and notify the physician.

82
00:12:51.397 --> 00:13:01.154
If it continues, move the clamp toward the collection system to isolate the leak; if it never stops, replace the system.

83
00:13:01.154 --> 00:13:05.382
Never leave a tube clamped with an ongoing air leak.

84
00:13:05.382 --> 00:13:13.107
If drainage holes show, the tube is partly out: notify the physician and never push it back in.

85
00:13:13.107 --> 00:13:18.880
Tube pulled out: occlusive dressing, firm pressure, call the physician.

86
00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:29.695
Removal criteria: drainage under one hundred milliliters in twenty-four hours, pneumothorax resolved on chest x-ray, and no air leak.

87
00:13:29.695 --> 00:13:36.525
For transport, keep the unit upright and below the chest, and never clamp routinely.

88
00:13:37.933 --> 00:13:46.147
Blunt trauma: an external force injures internal structures without necessarily breaking the skin.

89
00:13:46.147 --> 00:14:05.257
Motor vehicle crashes dominate; typical injuries are rib fractures, pulmonary contusions, pneumothorax, blunt cardiac injury, and aortic injury from rapid deceleration, commonly at the isthmus just distal to the left subclavian.

90
00:14:05.257 --> 00:14:10.202
Large diaphragm tears are diagnosed more often on the left.

91
00:14:10.202 --> 00:14:16.404
Fractures of ribs one and two, the scapula, or sternum signal great force.

92
00:14:16.404 --> 00:14:20.344
Penetrating trauma: an object enters the chest.

93
00:14:20.344 --> 00:14:35.095
Low-velocity stab wounds damage along the path; high-velocity rifle wounds, over fifteen hundred feet per second, cause extensive damage through energy transfer and cavitation.

94
00:14:35.095 --> 00:14:40.711
Cardiac tamponade and esophageal injury are most often penetrating.

95
00:14:40.711 --> 00:14:47.583
A blunt-trauma patient may look stable at first, then worsen as contusions evolve.

96
00:14:49.000 --> 00:15:06.178
The primary survey is ABCDE, ordered by rapidity and lethality: airway with cervical-spine protection, breathing, circulation with hemorrhage control, disability using GCS and pupils, and exposure while preventing hypothermia.

97
00:15:06.178 --> 00:15:13.474
The secondary survey is a head-to-toe exam with an AMPLE history once the patient is stabilized.

98
00:15:13.474 --> 00:15:52.770
Other lethal chest injuries: massive hemothorax, more than fifteen hundred milliliters of pleural blood, treated with chest tube drainage, blood products, and bleeding control; Hess adds that more than two hundred milliliters an hour for two to four hours points to emergent thoracotomy; cardiac tamponade, with Beck's triad of hypotension, JVD, and muffled heart sounds, though not always all three, treated by emergency pericardial decompression; and flail chest, three or more ribs fractured in two or more places.

99
00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:58.775
Pulmonary contusion may worsen over the first twenty-four to forty-eight hours.

100
00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:20.002
Epidural hematoma sits between dura and skull, is often arterial, and may show a lucid interval before sudden deterioration, though not in every patient; Hess adds it is biconvex on CT, often from the middle meningeal artery.

101
00:16:20.002 --> 00:16:33.556
Subdural hematoma sits between dura and arachnoid, usually from torn bridging veins, is more common, and Hess says the underlying injury is usually worse.

102
00:16:33.556 --> 00:16:37.164
Subarachnoid blood can trigger vasospasm.

103
00:16:37.164 --> 00:16:42.973
Diffuse axonal injury comes from shearing forces and may need MRI.

104
00:16:42.973 --> 00:16:50.982
To prevent secondary injury, watch for hypoxia, seen in twenty-two percent of TBI patients.

105
00:16:50.982 --> 00:17:07.000
Hess adds that SpO2 at or below ninety percent for a median twelve to twenty minutes independently predicted death, and a single systolic under ninety is linked to doubled mortality.

106
00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:16.433
Outside herniation, the goal is normocapnia, a PaCO2 around thirty-five to forty-five.

107
00:17:16.433 --> 00:17:30.258
Hyperventilation lowers PaCO2, constricts cerebral vessels, and temporarily drops ICP, but it also drops cerebral blood flow and can cause ischemia.

108
00:17:30.258 --> 00:17:41.187
The Trauma deck reserves it for impending herniation in severe TBI, to a PaCO2 below twenty-five, following protocol.

109
00:17:41.187 --> 00:17:53.517
The RCP 105 deck instead gives a twenty-six floor and a temporary thirty to thirty-five; for this exam, use the Trauma deck numbers.

110
00:17:53.517 --> 00:18:07.342
Mannitol pulls water out of the brain but, as a diuretic, may worsen hypotension; hypertonic saline draws water out and supports circulating volume.

111
00:18:07.342 --> 00:18:13.600
A new unilateral fixed, dilated pupil is a neurosurgical emergency.

112
00:18:15.033 --> 00:18:16.409
Pause and think.

113
00:18:16.409 --> 00:18:24.833
A postoperative patient develops sudden dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, and hypoxemia.

114
00:18:24.833 --> 00:18:29.475
CT pulmonary angiography confirms a pulmonary embolus.

115
00:18:29.475 --> 00:18:37.813
The physician asks for a drug that will dissolve the clot already lodged in the pulmonary artery.

116
00:18:37.813 --> 00:18:47.183
Which drug class is it, and what single lab value defines therapeutic anticoagulation for ongoing prevention?

117
00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:54.100
[6-second practice pause]

118
00:18:54.600 --> 00:19:08.545
Only thrombolytics dissolve existing clot; the common agent is tissue plasminogen activator, or tPA, and its bleeding risk must be weighed in a postoperative patient.

119
00:19:08.545 --> 00:19:19.214
Anticoagulants such as heparin, warfarin, or apixaban prevent further clot formation but do not dissolve what is already there.

120
00:19:19.214 --> 00:19:27.446
Anticoagulation is considered therapeutic when the PTT is two to two point five times the control.

121
00:19:27.446 --> 00:19:45.927
The management order: prophylaxis with intermittent pneumatic compression and early ambulation; anticoagulation; thrombolytics to dissolve clot; and an inferior vena cava filter to keep leg clots from reaching the lungs.

122
00:19:45.927 --> 00:20:01.048
Diagnosis is a V/Q scan showing perfusion defects with normal ventilation, or CT pulmonary angiography with contrast; the revised deck calls sudden dyspnea the most important sign.

123
00:20:01.048 --> 00:20:08.525
Virchow's triad explains the cause: stasis, vascular injury, and increased coagulability.

124
00:20:09.933 --> 00:20:11.103
ABCDE first.

125
00:20:11.103 --> 00:20:21.145
Pneumothorax: diminished or absent breath sounds and hyperresonance; the trachea shifts away, but late.

126
00:20:21.145 --> 00:20:28.457
Suspected tension with instability gets decompression before a chest x-ray.

127
00:20:28.457 --> 00:20:34.501
GCS equals E plus V plus M; eight or less and we think airway.

128
00:20:34.501 --> 00:20:49.515
CPP equals MAP minus ICP; keep it above sixty in trauma and avoid secondary injury from hypoxia, hypotension, hypercapnia, or high intrathoracic pressure.

129
00:20:49.515 --> 00:21:02.774
Chest tubes: match size to contents, insert over the rib in the safe triangle, water depth sets suction, tidaling means still connected.

130
00:21:02.774 --> 00:21:13.108
Postoperative PE: anticoagulant prevents, thrombolytic dissolves, PTT two to two point five times control.
